          ":question/id": "836e24be-b58e-500b-bb05-e9b2030ce5e6",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-133291",
          ":question/problem": "Find $y(t),$ the antiderivative of ${y}^{\u2032}(t).$\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "We know that ${y}^{\u2032}(t)={t}^{2}-2.$\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {t}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-2\\int {t}^{0}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2+1}}{2+1}\\right)-2t+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
        },
